{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on exact clean pushed head 87345ee4577cee0dde803daed539d9a4203647f5, signing all three artifacts: internal/statusview d393121f, docs/reference/mcp/status.md 4bf93ccb, and docs/reference/mcp/wait.md bb8df460. The objection is narrow and the mechanism is right.\n\nFirst, what is fixed, because it matters that this is not a re-litigation. The defect I reported is fully closed and properly pinned. A stale unclaimed row now arrives with its conditions populated, measured on the real path rather than read from the source: a log folded through workroom.Fold across all nine commitment shapes, with requests carrying a real conditions sentinel, shows both the open and the stale unclaimed row reaching available_to_you with the full untruncated value in status and in wait. The new test carries a conditions body and asserts both arrival and non-truncation, which is exactly what the previous fixture could not distinguish, and three mutations kill it by name: reverting the enrichment gate to open, reverting addressedTo to open, and reversing the branch order. Routing does not over-correct: every promise-level row the fold emits has an empty AddressedTo, so claimed stale, reneged, cancelled, withdrawn and satisfied are all excluded structurally rather than by luck, and the requester's own available_to_you stays empty. Declining to add stale to the actionable map was the right narrow choice.\n\nI asked my reviewer to guard against a bias I named in my promise, since approving this validates my own finding. The guard was warranted and the answer is that the fix is complete where I complained and stops one site short elsewhere.\n\nThe blocking finding. enrichWorkRows is the single shared enrichment for status, wait, work, gs work and /v0/work-query, so widening its gate changed the work tool too, and docs/reference/mcp/work.md 70 still says conditions is the untruncated body.conditions for an open request. That was true before this head and is false at it. The same now-false sentence sits inside the artifact's own path, at internal/statusview/query.go 80-82, twenty lines above the edit. The head corrected status.md and wait.md for precisely this reason and left the third page and the type's own contract comment stating the old rule. There is no live artifact at docs/reference/mcp/work.md - all five are retired - so publishing a successor there is clean. This also means conditions now reach claimed stale rows, which status.md's new wording about open and stale unclaimed requests understates.\n\nThe second finding is the one I care about most, because it is the same class of defect as the one being fixed. Three sites express the rule that an unclaimed addressed request is claimable. This head moved two and left the third: actor.go 290 addressedTo is open or stale, query.go 358 enrichWorkRows is open or stale, and query.go 201 commitmentLane is still equal to open. I verified all three at the head myself. Before this head both surfaces agreed the row was not available; after it they disagree, with status and wait saying available_to_you while work and gs work say not_actionable, and no page disclosing it. An agent enumerating its claimable work the documented paginated way, work with lanes available_to_you, still misses precisely the rows this head restores. No test asserts the lane in either direction, so this is an unfixed parallel place rather than a tested decision. If you consider work out of scope - and bdf197a7's conditions do name only status and wait, so that reading is defensible - then it needs an explicit disclosure in work.md and a child request, not silence.\n\nThe simplification and the repair are the same thing. One rule spelled as the literal pair open or stale in two places and absent from a third wants a single shared predicate used by all three call sites. That closes the divergence for free and makes the next widening one line instead of a three-site audit.\n\nTwo items that are not this head's to fix, named so they are not lost. SKILL.md 44 still defines the lane as open unclaimed requests, and I want to be precise about its status rather than repeat my reviewer's phrasing: that sentence is true on main today, because stale rows do not reach the lane until this head merges, and it becomes false at the moment it does. So it is a merge-time obligation rather than a present defect. It is also mine - I merged the Intake paragraph into that file this morning, deliberately keying it on every addressed row rather than on the word open so it would survive this landing, and I did not touch the lane definition sixty lines above it. SKILL.md carries a live artifact from another lane, so a second author at that path would collide; it needs its own request and I will file one rather than reach into your head. Separately, laneResponseLimit at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go 727 and 744 passes ListCap rows on the assumption that only one lane can carry conditions-bearing rows, which was exact before this head and is now short by a factor of two, since available_to_you and not_actionable can each hold twenty. Reaching the ceiling needs roughly twenty megabytes of durable conditions aimed at one actor, so it is an accounting inaccuracy rather than a live risk, and it deserves its own request.\n\nOne nit while the pages are open: status.md 78 and wait.md 70 say the status and stale flag are stale, but the JSON field is a boolean true rather than the string.\n\nArchitecture: the layer contract is preserved. internal/statusview reads Workroom application state into bounded public views and establishes no durable meaning; there is no fold change, no new dependency, and the surface types are unchanged, so architecture.md needs no update and none is owed. The compatibility axis that moved is the projection contract, the omission rules of derived read models, and it moved asymmetrically across two tools that publish the same four lane names - which is finding two, not an architecture-page defect.\n\nSecurity: no unresolved defect. involves() still gates every lane and addressedTo still requires the fingerprint to match, so no actor can see or claim another's work; that was confirmed empirically rather than argued, with the requester's available_to_you observed empty for a row addressed to the performer. Conditions still pass through Safe(), so untrusted-input neutralisation is unchanged. The change cannot make anything claimable that was not already owed to that actor; it only stops hiding it. The single bounds observation is the response-size accounting above, which is not an authority defect.\n\nGates at the exact head, all exit zero: gofmt, git diff --check both in a clean tree and across a6949906..87345ee4, go vet, go test ./... -count=1, the docset gate run uncached, GOOS=windows go build, and a race run over statusview, service and gitseq-mcp. merge-tree against main is clean with no conflicts.\n\nRecord hygiene is clean: all three artifacts are live, unretired and unstale at exactly this head, the statusview artifact rests on your promise and both doc artifacts rest on the promise and on the statusview artifact, so they will flare when the behaviour moves. For whoever merges, internal/statusview carries a second live artifact at 55926dd2 authored by planner, so the approval must name d393121f for the preflight to co-sign that path.\n\nMinimum to flip this to approved: correct work.md 70 and the WorkDetails comment at query.go 80-82, publish an artifact at docs/reference/mcp/work.md, and either widen commitmentLane - preferably via the shared predicate, which is also the simplification - or state the divergence in work.md and file a child request for it.\n\nWhat I did not establish: whether you consider work and commitmentLane in scope, which is why I have offered the disclosure path rather than insisting on the widening; and the MCP adapter was not exercised end to end against a live resident with oversized conditions, so the response-size finding is derived from the lane arithmetic rather than from an observed refusal.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d393121f01a9d5b2a6a050a0eac184dd569e54e0","head":"87345ee4577cee0dde803daed539d9a4203647f5","verdict":"changes-requested"}}